==Spanish==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|es|osp|-}}, from {{inh|es|VL.|*cocēre}}, from {{inh|es|la|coquere}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|coquō}}, from {{inh|es|itc-pro|*kʷekʷō}}, ultimately from {{der|es|ine-pro|*pekʷ-||to cook, become ripe}}. Some conjugated forms of the verb were reformed through analogy with the infinitive; in Old Spanish, the forms {{m|es|cueza}} and {{m|es|cuezo}} were ''cuega'' and ''cuego'', respectively, {{m|es|coció}} was ''coxo'', and the past participle was {{m|es|cocho}} instead of {{m|es|cocido}}<ref>https://www.scribd.com/doc/157209215/Diccionario-Critico-Etimologico-castellano-C-F-Corominas-Joan-pdf</ref>. Cognate with {{cog|en|cook}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{es-IPA}}
* {{homophones|coser|lang=es}} (''non-Castilian dialects'')

===Verb===
{{es-verb|coc|er|pres=cuezo}}

# to [[boil]]
#: {{ux|es|cocer a fuego lento|inline=1|[[simmer]]}}
# {{lb|es|reflexive|cocerse}} to [[brew]]
#: {{ux|es|Algo se está '''cociendo'''|[[Something is brewing]]}}
# {{lb|es|pronominal}} to [[chafe]] {{gloss|get sore}}
#: {{syn|es|[[escocer]]se|[[escaldar]]se}}

====Conjugation====
{{es-conj-er|c||p=-cer o-ue|combined=1}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|es|precocer}}

